    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "D3AT7",
      "x": 19390,
      "y": 46500,
      "name": "Advisor — states note",
      "width": 320,
      "height": 0,
      "content": "States\n\n• With data / empty — drawn (\"· empty\" frames).\n• Loading — the page keeps its head and toolbar and swaps the list for skeleton cards of the same height; the previous page stays on screen while the next one loads.\n• Error — an antd Alert in place of the list, with a retry action. No blank screen.\n\nSettings pages: fields keep their shape while loading (Spin over the card, as scout settings does); a failed save keeps the values and shows the error under the field."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "Q7aMs6",
      "x": -420,
      "y": 50000,
      "name": "[legacy] Row — Checkpoints",
      "width": 300,
      "height": 3100,
      "content": "Checkpoints\n\n28 days on one project with a stated goal and a verdict at the end. The author buys it. Every checkpoint has a curator, and the curator is either a person or the AI curator — one role, two kinds. A verdict of \"stage closed\" is what opens the next workflow stage; a stage takes one checkpoint or several.\n\nSpec: docs/checkpoints.md and docs/checkpoint-standard.md. Handoff: docs/design/specs/checkpoints.md.\n\nTHE PAGE IS A PATH\nA sidebar on the left holds the whole run in order — Prepare/Inputs, four sprints, the Checkpoint itself — and the panel on the right holds whatever is selected. It replaced a scrolling thread: a path can be looked at, a thread has to be read. The dates live in the sidebar, the day count in the header, the counts in the tabs, and nothing restates them.\n\nA SPRINT IS SEVEN DAYS, NOT A WEEK\nCounted from the day the checkpoint started, so it can run Tuesday to Monday. The UI never says \"week\".\n\nBUYING IT IS THE FIRST STEP OF THE PAGE\nPrepare and Inputs are one step. While the checkpoint is a draft the panel holds the five questions — what exists now, what must exist, who curates, when it starts, payment — and once it is paid the same step keeps the same content as a summary. No separate route, and nothing is entered twice. Cancel is the only thing that destroys a draft, and it confirms.\n\nCALLS OPEN SPRINTS\nCall 1 — the goal — is the first day of sprint 1, not a step before it. Until it fixes the goal card the lines on Inputs stay editable. The AI curator holds no calls at all.\n\nONE PANEL OF ASKS, ONE FORWARD BUTTON\nEvery step carries the same panel: what is still open, one row each, with an owner tag — you, the curator, the team — and the buttons that answer it. Its footer holds the one forward action: Inputs opens sprint 1, each sprint opens the next. Locked while anything is open, and it says what is missing. Next checkpoint is not that kind of button: it opens a new checkpoint at its Prepare step, bought like the first one.\n\nONE REVIEW PER SPRINT\nThe curator reviews the sprint, not the devlog — a sprint with no devlog still gets reviewed.\n\nEVIDENCE IS AN ARTIFACT BOUND TO A LINE\nThe project's tab stays Artifacts, the store; the sprint's tab is Evidence, what is bound to an outcome. One artifact can prove more than one.\n\nEXPLANATIONS ARE INFO ICONS\nNot sentences in the layout. On this canvas every info icon layer is named with the tooltip text it carries.\n\nTHE NUMBER RUNS THROUGH THE PROJECT\nCheckpoint 4 is the fourth on this project, whatever stage it belonged to. It is the first column of the past table and the first thing in every meta line; the stage follows it. Numbering that restarted inside a stage gave two runs the same name.\n\nMONEY IS SHOWN ONCE, BEFORE IT IS SPENT\nOn the payment question and in the curator picker. One number: the hours-by-rate line and the platform's share belong in the terms and the receipt. Nothing afterwards mentions a sum.\n\nPULSES COME FROM THE WORK\nA devlog or an artifact in that sprint confirms its author automatically. The cell has four faces plus one silence: confirmed, waiting, missed, not asked yet — and blank for a sprint somebody was not on the team, with the date on the row. A missed sprint and a sprint nobody was there for must never look alike.\n\nTWO TABLES, ONE TABLE\nPast checkpoints and Artifacts are DS · 11 / ModernTable: white card, 44 head on surface-sunken, 56 rows, border-subtle dividers, antd pagination. Head and body come from one column definition.\n\nWHAT THE SCHEMA CANNOT HOLD YET\nA review per sprint (it lives on the devlog row), the five calls (no time, link or held-at), a human mentor accepting and setting the day, and the fact a sprint was opened. See §8 of the handoff.\n\nComponents: Verdict status, Curator badge, Pulse cell, Evidence slot."
    },
